<!-- MODULE 5: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">For a high-consideration home product priced between $2,000 and $10,000, the path between first awareness and purchase rarely follows a straight line. Buyers research carefully: they compare suppliers, look for quality signals, assess craftsmanship and provenance, and revisit the brand over time before committing. Much of that evaluation happens away from Search.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Google Search captures buyers who have already concluded enough of that process to express intent explicitly through a keyword. It converts existing demand efficiently. What it cannot do, on its own, is build the next wave of that demand, which is why accounts that rely entirely on Search tend to reach a performance ceiling, even when they are otherwise managed well.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">When we inherited this account, Google was already working. A 3.1× blended ROAS on Search and Shopping, for a $2,000–$10,000 luxury home brand, is genuinely solid. The previous agency hadn't mismanaged it. They'd taken it about as far as a single channel can go.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">That's the part most clients don't see: there's a ceiling built into every channel-only strategy. Google Search converts the buyers who are already searching. It doesn't create them. When the pool of active searchers stops growing, ROAS plateaus, even if the account is being run well. The client's plateau wasn't a signal that something was broken. It was a signal that the account had already captured most of what was available.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">The constraint wasn't bidding or structure. It was demand. Nobody was building the cohort of buyers who would become next quarter's branded search traffic. That's what YouTube and Meta were actually for, not direct conversion, but seeding the next wave of high-intent search queries. Eighteen months later, with three channels sharing data and audiences, peak blended ROAS reached 7.0×.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 7: THE CHALLENGE -->
<section class="cs-challenge">
  <div class="cs-challenge-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">A well-run Google account at its ceiling with nothing building demand above it</h2>
    <p class="cs-para">The client's Google account was genuinely good. Better bidding and tighter match types had already been done. Their 3.1× wasn't the result of poor management, it was the result of a single-channel account that had already captured most of the search demand available to it.</p>
    <p class="cs-para">The real constraint: no one was building the pool of buyers who would become next quarter's branded search traffic. The same pond, year after year. When we laid it out that way, the client understood, but they were skeptical that YouTube and Meta could actually move Google's numbers. That skepticism was reasonable. It took 18 months of data to prove it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 8: WHAT WE FOUND -->
<section class="cs-findings">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">Three gaps the account didn't know it had</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">PMax was targeting cold traffic: no customer data had ever been uploaded</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">The brand had years of purchase history sitting in Klaviyo. None of it had been uploaded to Google. Performance Max was starting from zero on every campaign, with no reference point for what a real buyer looked like. We fixed this before spending a dollar on YouTube or Meta, so any new channel we launched would compound into an account already improving, not one still finding its footing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Nothing above the funnel: the pool of future searchers wasn't being replenished</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">No YouTube, no Meta, nothing building awareness. The client's skepticism was understandable: high-AOV luxury products don't convert cold from video or social. They were right about direct conversion. What upper-funnel channels actually do is build the audience that shows up in Google Search later, already familiar with the brand. That's the connection most single-channel accounts never make.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">The channels weren't sharing data: three channels running independently don't compound</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Adding YouTube and Meta doesn't automatically improve Google. The mechanism is data. YouTube viewer lists can be added to Google Ads as audience signals for Performance Max, a native integration within the Google ecosystem. First-party email data, collected through site visits and purchases across all channels, can be uploaded to Google as Customer Match. Without those connections, three channels just run in parallel. With them, each one informs the others.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">If Google is your only channel, you're working with a fixed pool of demand, with no visibility into whether the pool is shrinking. The Growth Diagnostic looks at whether there's untapped demand above the funnel and what it would take to connect it back into Google's bidding.</p>
      <a href="../growth-diagnostic-landing.html#book" class="cs-mid-hook-link">See what it covers &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

