<!-- MODULE 10: WHAT CHANGED -->
<section class="cs-interventions">
  <div class="cs-interventions-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What we built and in what order</h2>
    <p class="cs-para">We didn't launch YouTube and Meta first. We fixed Google first, so the new channels would compound into an account that was already improving, not a baseline still being corrected.</p>

    <div class="cs-intervention-list">
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Seeded PMax with verified customer history before launching any new channel</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">The brand's full purchase history was in Klaviyo. We segmented it by LTV, uploaded the top cohort to Google as Customer Match, and connected it to Performance Max as an audience signal. Google had a real reference point for what a buyer looked like before we spent a dollar elsewhere. The cold-start phase shortened. The baseline improved first.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Launched YouTube with brand lifestyle creative and a deliberate no-discount brief</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">No promotional messaging, no price anchoring, nothing that attracts bargain hunters to a $2,000–$10,000 product. In-stream and non-skippable formats, lifestyle framing only. YouTube viewer lists were added back to Google Ads as audience signals for Performance Max, not as a direct conversion path, but as a reference for the kinds of people who had already shown interest in the brand.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Built Meta TOF and BOF anchored to LTV-weighted Klaviyo lookalikes</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">The TOF lookalike seed wasn't a flat email list: it was the Klaviyo segment weighted by 6-month buyer LTV. BOF retargeted engaged site visitors. Both layers ran the same no-discount creative as YouTube. Email and visitor data generated across all channel activity flowed into Klaviyo and was re-uploaded to Google as Customer Match on a rolling basis, refreshing the audience signals over time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Aligned attribution windows across all three platforms to the actual purchase decision timeline</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Luxury home goods buyers don't convert in 7 days. The default platform windows were undercounting cross-channel contribution, with YouTube assists disappearing and Meta view-throughs not surfacing. We aligned windows to match the real decision timeline so the reported numbers across platforms were actually comparable and the cross-channel picture held together.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-callout">
      <p class="cs-callout-text">Someone who has seen you on YouTube and engaged with you on Meta is not the same Search click as someone finding you cold. The mechanism is data, not impression volume: those buyer signals move through your CRM into Google, where they inform how Smart Bidding weights the next auction. As the proportion of Google clicks from pre-warmed audiences grows, conversion efficiency tends to improve, not because anything in Google changed, but because the composition of who's clicking changed.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 12: THE OUTCOME -->
<section class="cs-outcome">
  <div class="cs-outcome-inner">
    <div class="cs-outcome-header">
      <h2 class="cs-h2">Eighteen months later: 3.1× Google-only to 7.0× peak cross-channel ROAS</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-results-grid" role="list" aria-label="Key results">
      <div class="cs-result" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-result-val">7.0&times;</span>
        <span class="cs-result-lbl">Peak Cross-Channel ROAS</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-result" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-result-val">3.1&times;</span>
        <span class="cs-result-lbl">Entry ROAS (Search Only)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-result" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-result-val">+126%</span>
        <span class="cs-result-lbl">Documented ROAS Improvement</span>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-result" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-result-val">18 mo.</span>
        <span class="cs-result-lbl">Build Period</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-outcome-conclusion">
      <p class="cs-para">The account went from a well-managed Google-only setup at 3.1× to a three-channel system peaking at 7.0×. No single change produced that number. The +126% compounded across 18 months as YouTube built the audience, Meta developed it, and Google converted it. The data layer connecting all three is what made the channels reinforce each other rather than simply coexist.</p>
      <p class="cs-para">The lift in Google's ROAS wasn't driven by anything we changed in Google's campaign structure. It came from a gradual shift in who was searching, as upper-funnel investment generated more queries from people already familiar with the brand, blended efficiency improved without touching bid targets or budgets.</p>
      <p class="cs-para">7.0× reflects peak blended ROAS over the 18-month measurement period; period average was lower and returns varied across months. Results depend on category, AOV, and the state of first-party data infrastructure before expanding to new channels. Brand identity withheld per client NDA.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

